{
  "publish_date": "2026-10-09",
  "topic_title": "Google's Gemini 'universal' workplace agent (Oct 8, 2026) — a practical playbook for automation consultants",
  "why_chosen": "Google announced a major agentic product push (Gemini agent) on Oct 8, 2026. This is a platform-level change that will affect how clients ask for automation, where execution happens, and how integrations are built — all core to Zeeshan’s AI automation, workflow consulting, n8n and SMB-focused pillars.",
  "angle": "Contrarian + practical: don’t treat agentic AI as an autonomous replacement for workflows. Treat Gemini (and similar agents) as an orchestration/assistant layer that must be wrapped with strict guardrails, observable execution, and workflow tools (e.g., n8n) to avoid vendor lock-in, security holes, and long-tail failures.",
  "opinion_direction_for_writer": "Be skeptical of ‘set it and forget it’ agent narratives. Argue that the early wins are in using agents as orchestrators (short-lived tasks, decision-support, API orchestration), not as unsupervised, long-running autonomous workers. Push for an n8n-first integration pattern (agent issues intents → n8n handles deterministic execution, retries, logging, idempotency, access control). End with a one-line provocation: \"Agentic AI is an opportunity — if you force it to play by the rules you already use for production automation.\"",
